instruction: 'Tell me how to season a wok'
target: "Seasoning a wok is a process that helps prevent food from sticking to the surface and adds non-stick properties over time. Here's a step-by-step guide on how to season a wok:\n\n1. Clean the wok: Before seasoning, make sure the wok is clean and free of any debris or residue. Wash it with soap and warm water, then dry it thoroughly with a towel.\n2. Apply a thin layer of oil: Using a paper towel, apply a thin, even layer of cooking oil (such as peanut or vegetable oil) to the entire surface of the wok, including the handle and underside. You can also use a silicone brush to spread the oil evenly.\n3. Heat the wok: Place the oiled wok over medium-high heat on your stovetop or electric stove. Let it heat up for 5-10 minutes, or until the oil starts to smoke slightly. This will help the oil penetrate the metal and create a chemical reaction that enhances non-stick properties.\n4. Wipe out excess oil: Once the wok has heated up, use a paper towel to wipe away any excess oil that has accumulated at the bottom of the pan. Leave a thin layer of oil on the surface.\n5. Repeat the process: For maximum non-stick performance, repeat steps 2-4 several times, allowing the wok to cool completely between each seasoning session.\n6. Store the wok: After seasoning, store the wok in a dry place, such as a hook or hanging rack. Avoid storing it in a humid environment or covering it with plastic wrap, which can trap moisture and compromise the seasoning.\n\nTips:\n\n* Use high-quality oil: Choose a pure, high-smoke-point oil like peanut or avocado oil for best results.\n* Don't overcrowd the wok: Seasoning works best when the wok is empty, so don't try to cook anything while seasoning.\n* Be patient: Seasoning a wok takes time and repetition. Don't rush through the process, and allow the wok to cool completely between sessions.\n\nBy following these steps, you'll be able to achieve a well-seasoned wok that will last for many years and provide excellent non-stick performance."
formatted_target: "<|start_header_id|>assistant<|end_header_id|>\n\nSeasoning a wok is a process that helps prevent food from sticking to the surface and adds non-stick properties over time. Here's a step-by-step guide on how to season a wok:\n\n1. Clean the wok: Before seasoning, make sure the wok is clean and free of any debris or residue. Wash it with soap and warm water, then dry it thoroughly with a towel.\n2. Apply a thin layer of oil: Using a paper towel, apply a thin, even layer of cooking oil (such as peanut or vegetable oil) to the entire surface of the wok, including the handle and underside. You can also use a silicone brush to spread the oil evenly.\n3. Heat the wok: Place the oiled wok over medium-high heat on your stovetop or electric stove. Let it heat up for 5-10 minutes, or until the oil starts to smoke slightly. This will help the oil penetrate the metal and create a chemical reaction that enhances non-stick properties.\n4. Wipe out excess oil: Once the wok has heated up, use a paper towel to wipe away any excess oil that has accumulated at the bottom of the pan. Leave a thin layer of oil on the surface.\n5. Repeat the process: For maximum non-stick performance, repeat steps 2-4 several times, allowing the wok to cool completely between each seasoning session.\n6. Store the wok: After seasoning, store the wok in a dry place, such as a hook or hanging rack. Avoid storing it in a humid environment or covering it with plastic wrap, which can trap moisture and compromise the seasoning.\n\nTips:\n\n* Use high-quality oil: Choose a pure, high-smoke-point oil like peanut or avocado oil for best results.\n* Don't overcrowd the wok: Seasoning works best when the wok is empty, so don't try to cook anything while seasoning.\n* Be patient: Seasoning a wok takes time and repetition. Don't rush through the process, and allow the wok to cool completely between sessions.\n\nBy following these steps, you'll be able to achieve a well-seasoned wok that will last for many years and provide excellent non-stick performance.<|eot_id|>"
